- not stated (card): Calibrated Stokes Q,U light curve files (taken from Wielgus et al. 2022); GRMHD library images (not public in paper); Exact numerical differentiation and segmentation details beyond three-point scheme; Smoothing spline handling of gaps/weights

### 2015ApJ...805..186L: Jet Motion, Internal Working Surfaces, and Nested Shells in the Protostellar System HH 212 (2015; star_formation; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What are the jet motion, shock structures (knots, internal working surfaces), nested CO shells and wiggle of the HH 212 protostellar jet, and is a wide-angle wind needed?
- step 2: Proper motion of knots
  - method: Compare SiO peak positions with convolved SMA map, aligned on continuum peaks [Proper Motion: "We first convolved our previous SMA SiO map to the resolution of our ALMA map and then aligned the two maps with the continuum peaks."]
  - assumption: jet in plane of sky so velocity ~ tangential velocity [Proper Motion: "Since the jet is almost in the plane of the sky, the jet velocity can be approximated to this tangential velocity."]
- result: Mean knot position shift = 0.36 ± 0.15 arcsec [Proper Motion: "the mean position shift is estimated to be $\sim$ $0^{\prime\prime}_{^\textrm{.}}36$$\pm$$0^{\prime\prime}_{^\textrm{.}}15$"]
- result: Jet tangential velocity = 115 ± 50 km/s [Proper Motion: "giving rise to a tangential velocity of $\sim$ 115$\pm50$ km s$^{-1}$"]
- limitation (authors): Southern jet not continuous beyond ~10 arcsec, so point symmetry unconfirmed [Jet Wiggle: "further observations are still needed to confirm that the wiggle of the jet is really point symmetric about the central source."]
- not stated (card): Calibration details, CASA version and calibrators (deferred to Lee et al. 2014); Cleaning parameters, masking, continuum subtraction; Method for measuring knot peak positions and map alignment; Fitting method for the wiggle model; Velocity-integration choices beyond those listed

### 2020A&A...636A..62K: Investigating the connection between γ-ray activity and the relativistic jet in 3C 273 during 2015-2019 (2020; agn_and_quasars; multi_facility_alma_minor)
- question: What is the origin of the 2015-2019 gamma-ray outbursts of 3C 273 and how do they connect to the parsec-scale jet?
- step 1: Build gamma-ray light curves
  - method: Unbinned likelihood analysis with 30-day, 7-day and 1-day bins, power-law model [2.1 Fermi-LAT: "As binning intervals for the $\gamma$-ray light curves, we select 30 and 7 days for the full time range (i.e., 2015–2019), and one day for flaring periods"]
  - software: ScienceTools
  - parameter: ROI 15x15 deg; zmax=90 deg; upper limits for TS<9 or dF/F>0.5 [2.1 Fermi-LAT: "We defined a region of interest (ROI) of $15^{\circ} \times 15^{\circ}$ centered at 3C 273"]
