  - method: Discrete correlation function with Gaussian fit to the peak; Pearson significance following Rani et al. 2013a [3.3 Correlation: "We employ the discrete correlation function (DCF; (cite: edelson1988)) to investigate the relationship between $\gamma$-ray and radio emission."]
  - parameter: DCF time steps 12 d (weekly) and 33 d (monthly) [3.3 Correlation: "We therefore use time steps of 12 and 33 days for the DCF analysis for the weekly and monthly binned $\gamma$-ray fluxes, respectively."]
  - fitting: Gaussian fit to DCF [Correlation between the radio and $\gam…: "To determine height and location of a DCF peak, we fit a Gaussian function defined as $DCF(t) = a\,\times$ exp$\left[-(t-c)^{2}/2w^{2}\right]$ to the DCF durve"]
  - validation: Varied DCF binning; repeated for 2015-2017 subset [3.3 Correlation: "However, the time delays found from Gaussian fits are only weakly affected by the binning of the DCF"]
- step 3: Analyze 43 GHz VLBA jet structure
  - method: Circular Gaussian components fitted in visibility domain; polarization maps; proper motions of the peak [3.4 Parsec-scale jet: "the structure of the jet was represented by a number of two-dimensional circular Gaussians fitted to the visibility domain."]
  - software: Difmap
  - parameter: 10% flux accuracy; position error ~1/10 beam; polarization threshold 9 sigma [2.3 VLBA 43 GHz: "We consider a conservative accuracy of flux densities of 10%."]
- result: DCF time lag, LAT 7d vs ALMA = -105 ± 4 days [Table 1: "$c$ (days) | $-105\pm4$ | $-112\pm6$"]
- result: DCF time lag, LAT 30d vs ALMA = -112 ± 6 days [Table 1: "$c$ (days) | $-105\pm4$ | $-112\pm6$"]
- result: Flux-index transition flux (monthly) = 1.03e-7 ± not stated ph cm^-2 s^-1 [3.2 Photon indices: "different trends above and below a flux of about $1.03\times10^{-7}$ ph cm$^{-2}$ s$^{-1}$"]
- result: Apparent speed after 2016 outburst = ~6.0 c ± not stated c [3.4 Parsec-scale jet: "The proper motion of this displacement is about 0.59 mas/year which corresponds to an apparent speed of $\sim$6.0 $c$"]
- result: S1 brightness temperature peak = 2.3e12 ± not stated K [4.2: "$S1$ reaches $2.3\times10^{12}$ K"]
